Aristotle launched this notion of potential of Infinity 
All right lets finish up with three theologians &amp; three radically different understandings of the Infinite

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:03.160
Lets go to the High Middle Ages the Scholastic Period &amp; the greatest of thinkers Thomas Aquinas

00:10:03.173 --> 00:10:08.393
Thomas Aquinas says as a Theologian I am interested in a different question

00:10:08.553 --> 00:10:15.513
U all are interested in quantity
I am interested in a quality of existence

00:10:15.613 --> 00:10:21.053
What mode of existence would God have that divine

00:10:21.053 --> 00:10:23.913
God would be qualitatively Infinite

00:10:23.913 --> 00:10:32.413
Which means God would be Insperfect thee si  mum 
The most perfect being

00:10:32.413 --> 00:10:38.473
It is a mode of existing a way of existing that sets God apart from anything else

00:10:38.493 --> 00:10:46.993
Not quantity 
So he bifurcates between the mathematical Infinite &amp; the religious or philosophical Infinite

00:10:46.993 --> 00:10:50.493
But still Aristotle rears his ugly head

00:10:50.493 --> 00:10:54.973
Because even this Infinite God couldn't create an Infinite object

00:10:54.973 --> 00:11:02.073
Because anything in the world must be by nature finite

00:11:02.073 --> 00:11:08.073
Moving on from Aristotle to the 15th century we find the Bishop of Cologne Nicholas of Cusa

00:11:08.093 --> 00:11:16.833
And here is a Theologian who decides he is gona go with the Infinite all the way

00:11:16.833 --> 00:11:18.833
No restraint

00:11:18.833 --> 00:11:20.700
So the Infinite what is that

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.393
It is that thing which has no limits

00:11:23.393 --> 00:11:28.273
Therefore every thing must be included within the Infinite

00:11:28.333 --> 00:11:30.733
Nothing can be excluded

00:11:30.733 --> 00:11:35.733
The world itself must be within God

